133	What’s New
134	Line 3a has been modified to clarify how a disregarded entity completes
135	this line. An LLC that is a disregarded entity should check the
136	appropriate box for the tax classification of its owner. Otherwise, it
137	should check the “LLC” box and enter its appropriate tax classification.
138	Cat. No. 10231X
139	
140	New line 3b has been added to this form. A flow-through entity is
141	required to complete this line to indicate that it has direct or indirect
142	foreign partners, owners, or beneficiaries when it provides the Form W-9
143	to another flow-through entity in which it has an ownership interest. This
144	change is intended to provide a flow-through entity with information
145	regarding the status of its indirect foreign partners, owners, or
146	beneficiaries, so that it can satisfy any applicable reporting
147	requirements. For example, a partnership that has any indirect foreign
148	partners may be required to complete Schedules K-2 and K-3. See the
149	Partnership Instructions for Schedules K-2 and K-3 (Form 1065).
150	
151	Purpose of Form
152	An individual or entity (Form W-9 requester) who is required to file an
153	information return with the IRS is giving you this form because they
154	Form W-9 (Rev. 3-2024)
155	
156	
157	Page 2
158	
159	Form W-9 (Rev. 3-2024)
160	
161	must obtain your correct taxpayer identification number (TIN), which
162	may be your social security number (SSN), individual taxpayer
163	identification number (ITIN), adoption taxpayer identification number
164	(ATIN), or employer identification number (EIN), to report on an
165	information return the amount paid to you, or other amount reportable
166	on an information return. Examples of information returns include, but
167	are not limited to, the following.
168	• Form 1099-INT (interest earned or paid).
169	• Form 1099-DIV (dividends, including those from stocks or mutual
170	funds).
171	• Form 1099-MISC (various types of income, prizes, awards, or gross
172	proceeds).
173	• Form 1099-NEC (nonemployee compensation).
174	• Form 1099-B (stock or mutual fund sales and certain other
175	transactions by brokers).
176	• Form 1099-S (proceeds from real estate transactions).
177	• Form 1099-K (merchant card and third-party network transactions).
178	• Form 1098 (home mortgage interest), 1098-E (student loan interest),
179	and 1098-T (tuition).
180	• Form 1099-C (canceled debt).
181	• Form 1099-A (acquisition or abandonment of secured property).
182	Use Form W-9 only if you are a U.S. person (including a resident
183	alien), to provide your correct TIN.
184	Caution: If you don’t return Form W-9 to the requester with a TIN, you
185	might be subject to backup withholding. See What is backup
186	withholding, later.
187	By signing the filled-out form, you:
188	1. Certify that the TIN you are giving is correct (or you are waiting for a
189	number to be issued);
190	2. Certify that you are not subject to backup withholding; or
191	3. Claim exemption from backup withholding if you are a U.S. exempt
192	payee; and
193	4. Certify to your non-foreign status for purposes of withholding under
194	chapter 3 or 4 of the Code (if applicable); and
195	5. Certify that FATCA code(s) entered on this form (if any) indicating
196	that you are exempt from the FATCA reporting is correct. See What Is
197	FATCA Reporting, later, for further information.
198	Note: If you are a U.S. person and a requester gives you a form other
199	than Form W-9 to request your TIN, you must use the requester’s form if
200	it is substantially similar to this Form W-9.
201	Definition of a U.S. person. For federal tax purposes, you are
202	considered a U.S. person if you are:
203	• An individual who is a U.S. citizen or U.S. resident alien;
204	• A partnership, corporation, company, or association created or
205	organized in the United States or under the laws of the United States;
206	• An estate (other than a foreign estate); or
207	• A domestic trust (as defined in Regulations section 301.7701-7).
208	Establishing U.S. status for purposes of chapter 3 and chapter 4
209	withholding. Payments made to foreign persons, including certain
210	distributions, allocations of income, or transfers of sales proceeds, may
211	be subject to withholding under chapter 3 or chapter 4 of the Code
212	(sections 1441–1474). Under those rules, if a Form W-9 or other
213	certification of non-foreign status has not been received, a withholding
214	agent, transferee, or partnership (payor) generally applies presumption
215	rules that may require the payor to withhold applicable tax from the
216	recipient, owner, transferor, or partner (payee). See Pub. 515,
217	Withholding of Tax on Nonresident Aliens and Foreign Entities.
218	The following persons must provide Form W-9 to the payor for
219	purposes of establishing its non-foreign status.
220	• In the case of a disregarded entity with a U.S. owner, the U.S. owner
221	of the disregarded entity and not the disregarded entity.
222	• In the case of a grantor trust with a U.S. grantor or other U.S. owner,
223	generally, the U.S. grantor or other U.S. owner of the grantor trust and
224	not the grantor trust.
225	• In the case of a U.S. trust (other than a grantor trust), the U.S. trust
226	and not the beneficiaries of the trust.
227	See Pub. 515 for more information on providing a Form W-9 or a
228	certification of non-foreign status to avoid withholding.
229	
230	Foreign person. If you are a foreign person or the U.S. branch of a
231	foreign bank that has elected to be treated as a U.S. person (under
232	Regulations section 1.1441-1(b)(2)(iv) or other applicable section for
233	chapter 3 or 4 purposes), do not use Form W-9. Instead, use the
234	appropriate Form W-8 or Form 8233 (see Pub. 515). If you are a
235	qualified foreign pension fund under Regulations section 1.897(l)-1(d), or
236	a partnership that is wholly owned by qualified foreign pension funds,
237	that is treated as a non-foreign person for purposes of section 1445
238	withholding, do not use Form W-9. Instead, use Form W-8EXP (or other
239	certification of non-foreign status).
240	Nonresident alien who becomes a resident alien. Generally, only a
241	nonresident alien individual may use the terms of a tax treaty to reduce
242	or eliminate U.S. tax on certain types of income. However, most tax
243	treaties contain a provision known as a saving clause. Exceptions
244	specified in the saving clause may permit an exemption from tax to
245	continue for certain types of income even after the payee has otherwise
246	become a U.S. resident alien for tax purposes.
247	If you are a U.S. resident alien who is relying on an exception
248	contained in the saving clause of a tax treaty to claim an exemption
249	from U.S. tax on certain types of income, you must attach a statement
250	to Form W-9 that specifies the following five items.
251	1. The treaty country. Generally, this must be the same treaty under
252	which you claimed exemption from tax as a nonresident alien.
253	2. The treaty article addressing the income.
254	3. The article number (or location) in the tax treaty that contains the
255	saving clause and its exceptions.
256	4. The type and amount of income that qualifies for the exemption
257	from tax.
258	5. Sufficient facts to justify the exemption from tax under the terms of
259	the treaty article.
260	Example. Article 20 of the U.S.-China income tax treaty allows an
261	exemption from tax for scholarship income received by a Chinese
262	student temporarily present in the United States. Under U.S. law, this
263	student will become a resident alien for tax purposes if their stay in the
264	United States exceeds 5 calendar years. However, paragraph 2 of the
265	first Protocol to the U.S.-China treaty (dated April 30, 1984) allows the
266	provisions of Article 20 to continue to apply even after the Chinese
267	student becomes a resident alien of the United States. A Chinese
268	student who qualifies for this exception (under paragraph 2 of the first
269	Protocol) and is relying on this exception to claim an exemption from tax
270	on their scholarship or fellowship income would attach to Form W-9 a
271	statement that includes the information described above to support that
272	exemption.
273	If you are a nonresident alien or a foreign entity, give the requester the
274	appropriate completed Form W-8 or Form 8233.
275	
276	Backup Withholding
277	What is backup withholding? Persons making certain payments to you
278	must under certain conditions withhold and pay to the IRS 24% of such
279	payments. This is called “backup withholding.” Payments that may be
280	subject to backup withholding include, but are not limited to, interest,
281	tax-exempt interest, dividends, broker and barter exchange
282	transactions, rents, royalties, nonemployee pay, payments made in
283	settlement of payment card and third-party network transactions, and
284	certain payments from fishing boat operators. Real estate transactions
285	are not subject to backup withholding.
286	You will not be subject to backup withholding on payments you receive
287	if you give the requester your correct TIN, make the proper certifications,
288	and report all your taxable interest and dividends on your tax return.
289	Payments you receive will be subject to backup withholding if:
290	1. You do not furnish your TIN to the requester;
291	2. You do not certify your TIN when required (see the instructions for
292	Part II for details);
293	3. The IRS tells the requester that you furnished an incorrect TIN;
294	4. The IRS tells you that you are subject to backup withholding
295	because you did not report all your interest and dividends on your tax
296	return (for reportable interest and dividends only); or
297	5. You do not certify to the requester that you are not subject to
298	backup withholding, as described in item 4 under “By signing the filledout form” above (for reportable interest and dividend accounts opened
299	after 1983 only).
300	
301	
302	Page 3
303	
304	Form W-9 (Rev. 3-2024)
305	
306	Certain payees and payments are exempt from backup withholding.
307	See Exempt payee code, later, and the separate Instructions for the
308	Requester of Form W-9 for more information.
309	See also Establishing U.S. status for purposes of chapter 3 and
310	chapter 4 withholding, earlier.
311	
312	What Is FATCA Reporting?
313	The Foreign Account Tax Compliance Act (FATCA) requires a
314	participating foreign financial institution to report all U.S. account
315	holders that are specified U.S. persons. Certain payees are exempt from
316	FATCA reporting. See Exemption from FATCA reporting code, later, and
317	the Instructions for the Requester of Form W-9 for more information.
318	
319	Updating Your Information
320	You must provide updated information to any person to whom you
321	claimed to be an exempt payee if you are no longer an exempt payee
322	and anticipate receiving reportable payments in the future from this
323	person. For example, you may need to provide updated information if
324	you are a C corporation that elects to be an S corporation, or if you are
325	no longer tax exempt. In addition, you must furnish a new Form W-9 if
326	the name or TIN changes for the account, for example, if the grantor of a
327	grantor trust dies.
328	
329	Penalties
330	Failure to furnish TIN. If you fail to furnish your correct TIN to a
331	requester, you are subject to a penalty of $50 for each such failure
332	unless your failure is due to reasonable cause and not to willful neglect.
333	Civil penalty for false information with respect to withholding. If you
334	make a false statement with no reasonable basis that results in no
335	backup withholding, you are subject to a $500 penalty.
336	Criminal penalty for falsifying information. Willfully falsifying
337	certifications or affirmations may subject you to criminal penalties
338	including fines and/or imprisonment.
339	Misuse of TINs. If the requester discloses or uses TINs in violation of
340	federal law, the requester may be subject to civil and criminal penalties.
341	
342	Specific Instructions
343	Line 1
344	You must enter one of the following on this line; do not leave this line
345	blank. The name should match the name on your tax return.
346	If this Form W-9 is for a joint account (other than an account
347	maintained by a foreign financial institution (FFI)), list first, and then
348	circle, the name of the person or entity whose number you entered in
349	Part I of Form W-9. If you are providing Form W-9 to an FFI to document
350	a joint account, each holder of the account that is a U.S. person must
351	provide a Form W-9.
352	• Individual. Generally, enter the name shown on your tax return. If you
353	have changed your last name without informing the Social Security
354	Administration (SSA) of the name change, enter your first name, the last
355	name as shown on your social security card, and your new last name.
356	Note for ITIN applicant: Enter your individual name as it was entered
357	on your Form W-7 application, line 1a. This should also be the same as
358	the name you entered on the Form 1040 you filed with your application.
359	• Sole proprietor. Enter your individual name as shown on your Form
360	1040 on line 1. Enter your business, trade, or “doing business as” (DBA)
361	name on line 2.
362	• Partnership, C corporation, S corporation, or LLC, other than a
363	disregarded entity. Enter the entity’s name as shown on the entity’s tax
364	return on line 1 and any business, trade, or DBA name on line 2.
365	• Other entities. Enter your name as shown on required U.S. federal tax
366	documents on line 1. This name should match the name shown on the
367	charter or other legal document creating the entity. Enter any business,
368	trade, or DBA name on line 2.
369	• Disregarded entity. In general, a business entity that has a single
370	owner, including an LLC, and is not a corporation, is disregarded as an
371	entity separate from its owner (a disregarded entity). See Regulations
372	section 301.7701-2(c)(2). A disregarded entity should check the
373	appropriate box for the tax classification of its owner. Enter the owner’s
374	name on line 1. The name of the owner entered on line 1 should never
375	be a disregarded entity. The name on line 1 should be the name shown
376	on the income tax return on which the income should be reported. For
377	
378	example, if a foreign LLC that is treated as a disregarded entity for U.S.
379	federal tax purposes has a single owner that is a U.S. person, the U.S.
380	owner’s name is required to be provided on line 1. If the direct owner of
381	the entity is also a disregarded entity, enter the first owner that is not
382	disregarded for federal tax purposes. Enter the disregarded entity’s
383	name on line 2. If the owner of the disregarded entity is a foreign person,
384	the owner must complete an appropriate Form W-8 instead of a Form
385	W-9. This is the case even if the foreign person has a U.S. TIN.
