581	Line 6
582	Enter your city, state, and ZIP code.
583	
584	Part I. Taxpayer Identification Number (TIN)
585	Enter your TIN in the appropriate box. If you are a resident alien and
586	you do not have, and are not eligible to get, an SSN, your TIN is your
587	IRS ITIN. Enter it in the entry space for the Social security number. If you
588	do not have an ITIN, see How to get a TIN below.
589	If you are a sole proprietor and you have an EIN, you may enter either
590	your SSN or EIN.
591	If you are a single-member LLC that is disregarded as an entity
592	separate from its owner, enter the owner’s SSN (or EIN, if the owner has
593	one). If the LLC is classified as a corporation or partnership, enter the
594	entity’s EIN.
595	Note: See What Name and Number To Give the Requester, later, for
596	further clarification of name and TIN combinations.
597	How to get a TIN. If you do not have a TIN, apply for one immediately.
598	To apply for an SSN, get Form SS-5, Application for a Social Security
599	Card, from your local SSA office or get this form online at
600	www.SSA.gov. You may also get this form by calling 800-772-1213. Use
601	Form W-7, Application for IRS Individual Taxpayer Identification
602	Number, to apply for an ITIN, or Form SS-4, Application for Employer
603	Identification Number, to apply for an EIN. You can apply for an EIN
604	online by accessing the IRS website at www.irs.gov/EIN. Go to
605	www.irs.gov/Forms to view, download, or print Form W-7 and/or Form
606	SS-4. Or, you can go to www.irs.gov/OrderForms to place an order and
607	have Form W-7 and/or Form SS-4 mailed to you within 15 business
608	days.
609	If you are asked to complete Form W-9 but do not have a TIN, apply
610	for a TIN and enter “Applied For” in the space for the TIN, sign and date
611	the form, and give it to the requester. For interest and dividend
612	payments, and certain payments made with respect to readily tradable
613	instruments, you will generally have 60 days to get a TIN and give it to
614	the requester before you are subject to backup withholding on
615	payments. The 60-day rule does not apply to other types of payments.
616	You will be subject to backup withholding on all such payments until
617	you provide your TIN to the requester.
618	Note: Entering “Applied For” means that you have already applied for a
619	TIN or that you intend to apply for one soon. See also Establishing U.S.
620	status for purposes of chapter 3 and chapter 4 withholding, earlier, for
621	when you may instead be subject to withholding under chapter 3 or 4 of
622	the Code.
623	Caution: A disregarded U.S. entity that has a foreign owner must use
624	the appropriate Form W-8.
