792	Secure Your Tax Records From Identity Theft
793	Identity theft occurs when someone uses your personal information,
794	such as your name, SSN, or other identifying information, without your
795	permission to commit fraud or other crimes. An identity thief may use
796	your SSN to get a job or may file a tax return using your SSN to receive
797	a refund.
798	To reduce your risk:
799	• Protect your SSN,
800	• Ensure your employer is protecting your SSN, and
801	• Be careful when choosing a tax return preparer.
802	If your tax records are affected by identity theft and you receive a
803	notice from the IRS, respond right away to the name and phone number
804	printed on the IRS notice or letter.
805	If your tax records are not currently affected by identity theft but you
806	think you are at risk due to a lost or stolen purse or wallet, questionable
807	credit card activity, or a questionable credit report, contact the IRS
808	Identity Theft Hotline at 800-908-4490 or submit Form 14039.
809	For more information, see Pub. 5027, Identity Theft Information for
810	Taxpayers.
811	
812	
813	Page 6
814	
815	Form W-9 (Rev. 3-2024)
816	
817	Victims of identity theft who are experiencing economic harm or a
818	systemic problem, or are seeking help in resolving tax problems that
819	have not been resolved through normal channels, may be eligible for
820	Taxpayer Advocate Service (TAS) assistance. You can reach TAS by
821	calling the TAS toll-free case intake line at 877-777-4778 or TTY/TDD
822	800-829-4059.
823	Protect yourself from suspicious emails or phishing schemes.
824	Phishing is the creation and use of email and websites designed to
825	mimic legitimate business emails and websites. The most common act
826	is sending an email to a user falsely claiming to be an established
827	legitimate enterprise in an attempt to scam the user into surrendering
828	private information that will be used for identity theft.
829	The IRS does not initiate contacts with taxpayers via emails. Also, the
830	IRS does not request personal detailed information through email or ask
831	taxpayers for the PIN numbers, passwords, or similar secret access
832	information for their credit card, bank, or other financial accounts.
833	If you receive an unsolicited email claiming to be from the IRS,
834	forward this message to phishing@irs.gov. You may also report misuse
835	of the IRS name, logo, or other IRS property to the Treasury Inspector
836	General for Tax Administration (TIGTA) at 800-366-4484. You can
837	forward suspicious emails to the Federal Trade Commission at
838	spam@uce.gov or report them at www.ftc.gov/complaint. You can
839	contact the FTC at www.ftc.gov/idtheft or 877-IDTHEFT (877-438-4338).
840	If you have been the victim of identity theft, see www.IdentityTheft.gov
841	and Pub. 5027.
842	Go to www.irs.gov/IdentityTheft to learn more about identity theft and
843	how to reduce your risk.
